 ## Deciding each element
 
-**Palette.** Start neutral, then add colour only for roles the product needs (act, follow/cite, caution, failure, health, data categories). Take each colour from the brand when it fits the role; if the brand lacks a role, choose a colour that sits naturally with the brand (often a related hue at a different lightness, or the brand's own secondary), not a fashionable accent. Design dark mode as its own palette with the same roles. Check contrast for every text use.
+**Palette.** Choose a palette for the product’s character and tasks. Keep operational signals clear; identity, context and atmosphere are also valid roles. Take each colour from the brand when it fits the role; if the brand lacks a role, choose a colour that sits naturally with the brand (often a related hue at a different lightness, or the brand's own secondary), not a fashionable accent. Design dark mode as its own palette with the same roles. Check contrast for every text use.
 
-**Type.** System fonts when the product should feel native to its host or when speed and rendering matter; a brand or characterful face when the product is a destination or a brand surface. One scale, few sizes, weight before size for hierarchy, tabular numerals where numbers align.
+**Type.** System fonts when the product should feel native to its host or when speed and rendering matter; a brand or characterful face when the product is a destination or a brand surface. Choose size, weight and measure together to make the hierarchy clear; use tabular numerals where numbers align.
 
-**Shape and space.** Pick a corner and spacing language that suits the character (tight and precise, soft and friendly) and apply it consistently: nested shapes take smaller radii than their containers; one gutter; a short spacing scale.
+**Shape and space.** Choose a related family of shapes and insets for the component roles. Judge curve, corner extent, dimensions and padding together at actual size. Nested shapes need a convincing gap, not simply the same token or an automatic smaller radius. For a soft continuous treatment, consult the optional `shape-language.md`; it is one visual choice, not the philosophy’s default.
 
-**Elevation and surfaces.** Flat by default; elevation only for what floats over content (popovers, a docked input, a dragged item). Hairlines before cards; a filled panel only for a genuinely grouped fact.
+**Elevation and surfaces.** Use surfaces, borders, space and elevation to make grouping and depth clear. A flat list may fit dense data; an inset object may help a person recognize and act on a meaningful thing. Judge the hierarchy and density rather than counting cards.
 
 **Motion.** Decide one easing and two or three durations. Motion explains change (arrival, growth, focus moving); loops only while work is happening.
 
